      "rev_n_7_4989f6_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_4989f6_1",
        "node_id": "n_7_4989f6",
        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error occurs because the code assumes that numpy is available, but it's not in the execution environment. Let's modify the code to use only built-in Python libraries to solve this problem without relying on external libraries.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "6605170024613090959",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_7_4989f6_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_98cf6120b8",
        "created_at": 1794533159.2686887
      },
      "rev_n_6_3b60e8_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_3b60e8_1",
        "node_id": "n_6_3b60e8",
        "thought_text": "The current approach is flawed because it doesn't correctly model the random walk in the woods. Instead of considering all possible paths and their probabilities, it should use a Markov chain approach to calculate the expected time. The correct approach is to build the transition matrix based on the given paths and then use the formula for expected time in Markov chains: E[T] = 1 + sum(P(i, exit) * d(i, exit)) for all i, where P(i, exit) is the probability of reaching the exit from node i, and d(i, exit) is the shortest distance from node i to the exit.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "6025528221908898748",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_6_3b60e8_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_e68c64ade2",
        "created_at": 1794533209.9601333
